<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Saqqawist Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1929 CE – 1929 CE · capital Kabul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The nine-month rule of Habibullah Kalakani, a Tajik rebel who seized Kabul from King Amanullah in 1929.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan's</a> modernising reforms provoked tribal and religious revolts in late 1928. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habibullah-kalakani">Habibullah Kalakani</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajik</a> bandit leader from the Shomali plain north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, known as Bacha-i Saqao, 'son of the water carrier', captured Kabul in January 1929, and Amanullah abdicated and fled.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habibullah-kalakani">Kalakani</a> proclaimed himself emir as Habibullah II and reversed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah</a>'s reforms, closing girls' schools and restoring religious law, with backing from conservative clergy. His regime controlled little beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and the north and was unrecognised by most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtun</a> tribes, who resented a non-Pashtun ruler.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-nadir-shah">Mohammad Nadir Khan</a>, a former general, raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtun</a> tribes and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in October 1929. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habibullah-kalakani">Kalakani</a> was captured and executed in November, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nadir Shah</a> restored the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-afghanistan">Kingdom of Afghanistan</a>. Kalakani has been rehabilitated by some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajik</a> nationalists.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saqqawist-emirate.jpg" alt="Saqqawist Emirate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: ThrashedParanoid and Orange Tuesday. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Afghanistan_(1929).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1929 CE – 1929 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kabul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Habibullah Kalakani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Afghanistan (1929), Saqqawist Afghanistan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1929&amp;focus=saqqawist-emirate" aria-label="Saqqawist Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6437 927 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saqqawist Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6757 1183L6757 1183"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6757 1183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kabul. <a href="/atlas?year=1929&amp;focus=saqqawist-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
